fact WorkItem(id: "WI-1037", description: some(value: "A rule body reaching a supplied spec-op implementation whose PARENT SORT declares `requires` still runs the spec's DEFAULT. INDEPENDENTLY DELIVERABLE — the three-part fix below is entirely inside `reduce_op_value`, and the bridge already resolves the impl's own `requires` chain (WI-300 Tier B). WI-1040 (the relational `requires`) is needed only for the narrower case where the ENCLOSING RULE has its own requirement to forward; see `docs/design/requirement-channel.md` §7.\n\nTHE SITE: WI-1026 made a rule-body call on a defaulted spec op honour the typer's pin (`NodeOccurrence::classified_apply_target`, read by `reduce_op_value`), but that reader answers for `CallClass::PinNow` ONLY. `classify_pin_or_apply_within` writes `ConcreteApplyWithin` instead whenever `sort_reads_requirement_slots(impl_sort)` — exactly when the supplied implementation's own sort has `requires` — and that variant means the callee needs a dispatch dictionary in its frame. `reduce_op_value` folds STRUCTURALLY with no frame, so redirecting to it would inline a body whose `requires` slot nothing filled. WHY THE SHARED READER USED TO NAME IT ANYWAY: eval's copy (the one WI-1026 lifted) did list `ConcreteApplyWithin`, but eval's `Expr::Apply` arm matches that variant THREE ARMS EARLIER and routes it to `start_apply_same_sort`, which installs the dispatch_dict — so the branch was DEAD in eval and had never been exercised. It is not dead for the resolver, which calls the reader unconditionally. WI-1026 narrowed the reader to PinNow and recorded this gap rather than shipping a body run without its dictionary.\n\nTWO CORRECTIONS to this ticket's original WORK sentence, both established by reading the code 2026-08-07; the original plan would have shipped a WRONG ANSWER.\n\n(1) \"Let the depth-0 eval bridge take it\" is NOT sufficient and as written is HARMFUL. `bridge_op_to_eval` is handed `op`, and `reduce_op_value` computes `op = occ.classified_apply_target().unwrap_or(functor)` — which for `ConcreteApplyWithin` is `None`, so `op` is the SPEC op. The bridge entry runs the symbol it is given: `call_op_bridged` -> `invoke_op_with_requirements` (`eval/mod.rs:904`) pushes `cached_operation_body(sym)` straight into a frame, and the WI-444 value-directed override (`resolve_carrier_override_by_value`) lives in `dispatch_resolved_operation`, the IN-BODY dispatch path, NOT the entry. So routing to the bridge unchanged would run the spec's DEFAULT inside a frame with real dictionaries installed — today's wrong answer, upgraded to a definite one. The fix must redirect the callee to `ConcreteApplyWithin.fn_target_sym` BEFORE computing `params` and calling the bridge; `resolve_bridge_requirements` then keys on the IMPL's parent chain, which is precisely the `requires` that made the classification `ConcreteApplyWithin`.\n\n(2) \"The bridge already residualizes when a body needs a dict it cannot supply (WI-625 gap 3)\" describes PRE-Tier-B behaviour. Gap 3 was CLOSED by WI-300 Tier B: `call_op_bridged` (`eval/mod.rs:584`) resolves REAL dictionaries at the concrete argument types via `resolve_bridge_requirements` (`typing.rs:14667`), and residualize is only the fallback for `Unresolvable` / `Ambiguous`. So the reachable outcome here is a correct ANSWER, not merely \"delays instead of wrong\" — the acceptance below is stronger than originally filed.\n\nMEASURE FIRST — this ticket asserts two different current behaviours and BOTH can be true, for different fixtures; establish which before writing the arm. A DEFAULTED spec op folds its default (wrong value). An ABSTRACT spec op hits `op_body_node -> None -> return v` at `resolve.rs:6136`, which returns BEFORE the bridge arm, so it never bridges at all and the goal delays (answers `[]`). If the fix routes through the bridge, that early return is a SECOND site to change, not just the `match reduced` arm.\n\nTHE FIX, three parts, all in `reduce_op_value`: (a) redirect the callee to `ConcreteApplyWithin.fn_target_sym` before computing `params`; (b) decline the structural fold for that class rather than folding the spec default; (c) reach the bridge, including from the abstract-op early return at `resolve.rs:6136`. `resolve_bridge_requirements` then builds the impl's own chain at the concrete argument types. The outcome that becomes unreachable is the current one: silently running the spec's default. Only when the ENCLOSING RULE declares a requirement to forward does this need WI-1040's supply path, and that case can follow separately.\n\nACCEPTANCE: a rule body whose supplied impl lives in a `requires`-declaring sort answers THAT IMPL (delay only when the carrier is genuinely unbound), never the spec default; the abstract-spec-op fixture is measured and its behaviour stated at the test site; the WI-1026 tests still pass. Each test says which case fails when the change is backed out — a test that passes both with and without the fix measures nothing here, since the default and the impl must return DIFFERENT values for the assertion to have any content.\n\nREFERENCE: WI-1040; `docs/design/requirement-channel.md`; WI-1026; WI-444; WI-829; `NodeOccurrence::classified_apply_target` carries the reasoning at the site."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-08-09T19:28:48Z"))

fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-1037", author: "user", content: "IMPLEMENTATION GUARD (/code-review 2026-08-07 finding 7): if the fix adds a fourth CallClass variant, note that BOTH decode sites read CallClass through catch-alls — classified_apply_target (node_occurrence.rs:608, _ => None) and eval classification match — so a new variant lands SILENTLY in both engines catch-alls with no compile error: eval start_applies the spelled spec op with NO dictionary and the resolver folds the spec default, the precise failure class this ticket exists to remove. Make both decodes exhaustive (or return a typed verdict Pin(sym) | NeedsDict | Unclassified) — the same file carry_typer_stamps_from already made its destructuring exhaustive for exactly this reason (a fifth typer-written cell is a compile error). Also note WI-1044 (fired 2026-08-07) DEPENDS on this ticket: its query-path stamp creates a ConcreteApplyWithin population on the reduce path that must decline the fold and reach the bridge.", at: "2026-08-07T10:52:58Z")

fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-1037", author: "user", content: "DELIVERED with the ticket's own part (c) already done and one NEW hazard created by the fix, both measured.\n\n(1) PART (c) WAS ALREADY DELIVERED BY WI-1057. The ticket's MEASURE FIRST names two current behaviours; the ABSTRACT one was stale. It predicted an abstract spec op 'hits op_body_node -> None -> return v ... so it never bridges at all and the goal delays (answers [])' and named that early return as a SECOND site to change. Measured before writing anything: abstract/RULE-BODY answers 7 already, and so does abstract/OP-BODY. Verified the attribution rather than assumed it -- with WI-1057's arm alone disabled (None if false && dispatch_body_less && ...) the row answers [] exactly as predicted, and restoring it brings back 7. So this change is (a) + (b) only. The DEFAULTED row was exactly as filed: 1, the spec default.\n\n(2) THE FIX IS A TYPE, NOT A WIDER READ, and the ticket's correction (1) is why. classified_apply_target merged 'which operation' with 'may I enter it bare' and answered only the first; widening it to name ConcreteApplyWithin would inline a body whose requires slot nothing filled -- a second wrong answer, not a fix. ApplyDispatch::NeedsDict(impl) carries both, reduce_op_value declines the fold and bridges. Both decodes are now exhaustive over CallClass, which is this ticket's own implementation-guard feedback.\n\n(3) THE NEW BRIDGE IS NOT depth==0 GATED, and that is the op-body row's doing. The two arms beside it gate on 'a depth-0 bridge covers nested calls'; for this class the premise is FALSE -- eval reaches a nested ConcreteApplyWithin via start_apply_same_sort, which installs only the classification's dispatch_dict, and that is None for a cross-sort call from a namespace-level caller (WI-415's recorded gap). Riding the enclosing bridge raised 'DeferToRequirement: requirement param __req_tag not bound in caller frame' and tripped bridge_op_to_eval's internal-error debug_assert. That row is a test now.\n\n(4) NAMING THE CLASS CREATED A HAZARD -- found by /code-review, fixed here, and it is the one thing neither the ticket nor its guard predicted. reduce_op_value's Expr::ApplyWithin arm does not RETURN a decision: it rebuilds the call on the member the DICTIONARY selected and RECURSES, and rebuilt_expr carries the CallClass verbatim (carry_typer_stamps_from). So the rebuilt node reaches the Expr::Apply decode still carrying the spec-op site's stamp, and once the decode names ConcreteApplyWithin the static pin OVERRIDES the member the caller's dictionary just chose -- the inverse of what WI-1040's weave exists for. While the decode answered None this was invisible: the rebuild fell through to its own functor, which IS the target, so the dictionary won BY ACCIDENT. The arm now re-stamps PinNow(target). My first comment at the sibling site asserted the two mechanisms 'cannot race because reduce_op_value returns on Expr::ApplyWithin before it reads an Expr::Apply's classification' -- FALSE, it recurses; the review refuted it and the comment is corrected.\n\n(5) RE-MEASURED WHAT BRIDGE_REENTRY_CAP's DOC ASKS FOR, since this adds a third bridge call site and the only un-depth-gated one. UNCHANGED: the WI-1057 fixture still overflows at 1 MiB and passes at 1.5 MiB, clearing libtest's 2 MiB with the same margin. TRAP FOR THE NEXT MEASURER: setting RUST_MIN_STACK on 'cargo test' measures RUSTC's stack, not the test's, and reports an identical threshold whatever the code does -- my first attempt at this measurement was invalid for that reason and would have 'proved' the change was free. Run the built binary.\n\n(6) TWO SIBLING READERS STAMPED, not changed. collect_covered_calls keeps the narrow read (14 corpus occurrences reach it as NeedsDict and are woven; the re-stamp in (4) is what makes that safe) -- narrowing it to apply_dispatch() != Unclassified is a live choice about which route owns them, and wi1045 is what would move. dispatched_relation_arity keeps it too, with its note corrected: 'arity only' was wrong, since functional_relation_arity also applies the effect-free clause, which for this class lands on the SPEC and not the target (WI-453's reason for reading it off the target). Reachable outcome is a delay, not a wrong answer -- the bridge's scratch interpreter has an empty effect registry.\n\n(7) bridge_op_to_eval's doc asserted the OPPOSITE of what it does ('only requirement-FREE ops decide', WI-625 gap 3) and has been stale since WI-300 Tier B. Corrected at the site, because this fix depends on it being false.\n\nTwo SIGSEGVs during verification were environmental (the memory-pressure window right after clearing a 62 GB incremental cache), not the change: the new branch fires ZERO times in the binary that died, and it survived 20/20 standalone runs. Full workspace: 29 binaries, 4447 passed, 0 failed.", at: "2026-08-09T19:29:21Z")
